            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Direct Conclusion: The Root Cause of AI Ad Copy Homogenization is 'Prompt Convergence'</h2>
<p>Many operators report that after adopting AI tools, copy generation speed improved, but CTR actually dropped. It's not that AI doesn't work—it's that <strong>when大量投放者使用相似的Prompt structures, AI converges to the same 'optimal solution'</strong>, producing template-like expressions that sound correct but lack distinctive character.</p>
<p>This isn't an AI flaw; it's homogenization in usage methodology.</p>
<h2>Why Your AI Copy Keeps 'Colliding' with Competitors</h2>
<h3>1. Prompt Lacks Differentiation Dimensions</h3>
<p>Common Prompts look like this:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>"Write 5 Facebook ad copies for a shopping App, highlight limited-time discounts, emphasize user pain points, add CTA at the end"</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This instruction works for any product. AI can only produce "safe but mediocre" content.</p>
<h3>2. Results Used Directly Without Secondary Processing</h3>
<p>Under tight operational schedules, many teams throw AI output directly into ad groups. One utility App team once used AI to batch-generate 80 copies, only to see CTR drop 23% compared to human-written ones. After review, they found "Get Started Free Today" appeared in competitor creatives over 60% of the time.</p>
<h3>3. Over-Trusting 'Optimal Expression'</h3>
<p>AI training data comes from publicly published high-performing ads. When all AI tools study the same cases, output inevitably converges. You think you're using "proven expressions," but you're actually copying the same assignment as competitors.</p>
<h2>4 Strategies to Break Through Homogenization</h2>
<h3>Strategy 1: Give AI a 'Character Background'</h3>
<p>Add specific persona constraints in the Prompt:</p>
<ul>
<li>Not "write a promotional copy," but "write a WeChat Moments-style ad for a 25-year-old digital enthusiast familiar with 3C products"</li>
<li>Include brand speaking habits, catchphrases, emoji preferences</li>
<li>Specify audience age, profession, specific use scenarios</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Core logic</strong>: The more specific constraints, the smaller—yet more unique—AI's output space becomes.</p>
<h3>Strategy 2: Add a 'Negative List' in Prompt</h3>
<p>Explicitly tell AI what NOT to do:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>"Avoid expressions like 'unleash your potential' 'limited-time offer' 'don't miss out', avoid exclamation marks, keep titles under 8 characters"</p>
</blockquote>
<p>One cross-border e-commerce team found that adding a negative list reduced copy overlap with competitors from 41% to 12%.</p>
<h3>Strategy 3: AI Generation + Human Injection of 'Counter-Intuition'</h3>
<p>AI excels at producing logically sound conventional expressions, but <strong>user attention is often grabbed by counter-intuitive information</strong>.</p>
<p>Recommended workflow:<br />
1. AI generates 3-5 conventional versions<br />
2. Humans select 1-2 and brainstorm a "counter-intuitive" angle<br />
3. Have AI rewrite based on the new angle</p>
<p>For example, AI generated "time-saving project management tool," you inject the counter-intuition: "We're not about finishing work faster—we're about doing 50% less."</p>
<h3>Strategy 4: Use AI as 'Translator' Not 'Creator'</h3>
<p>Reposition AI from creation to translation: you provide core selling points, AI handles multi-language adaptation, multi-platform rewriting, multi-version testing.</p>
<p>This suits large-scale overseas teams better—core creativity stays human-controlled, AI handles execution-level scaling.</p>
<h2>A Final Reminder</h2>
<p>AI copy homogenization fundamentally stems from <strong>Prompt design homogenization</strong>. The tool isn't wrong; the usage approach determines outcomes.</p>
<p>I suggest checking your team's common Prompts today and adding "negative lists" and "character backgrounds." Observe data changes over 3-5 days. Practice proves truth—operational experience always runs ahead of algorithms.</p>
